# Every node of every graph the filings make, with what that graph's nodes carry. One row per
# (graph, node).
#
# A node must be joinable
#   That means the edges must not lose the key first. A node set taken from the ends of an edge list
#   whose keys are already joined into text is a set of display strings: nothing can be joined to
#   it, so a drawing of the layer graph could carry no fact about a layer except its name, and a
#   renderer with no order to read draws its own placeholder.
#
# Join at the real grain, join the key into text afterwards
#   The order of operations is the whole point. The layer arm joins `rank/evaluation_order.sqlc` on
#   `(filing, layer)`, the key `diagrams/lane_grain.sqlc` asserts is whole, and only then joins it
#   into text for the drawing's node id. `rank/graph_edges.sqlc` does it first, because everything
#   downstream of it counts. The same idiom in the opposite order, and the order decides whether a
#   fact can still be reached.
#
# `rank` on the unit arm is `notApplicable`
#   It is NULL there, and that is `notApplicable`, not `unmeasured`. The order is the deepest
#   descent through the parts, an order on layers. A unit is not a layer and has no place in that
#   order, so no number is missing here and none is worth looking for. A renderer must draw no label
#   rather than a number, because printing one gives an order to something the model gives none,
#   which is worse than printing nothing.
#
# What could refute each layer
#   The second fact a layer node carries is how much of this repository can contradict it.
#   `rank/layer_reach.sqlc` counts the rules that examine each layer, and `violated` is how many of
#   them said no. Joined here, the drawing of the layer graph stops being a picture of what was
#   filed and becomes a picture of what could be refuted, which is the only thing that separates a
#   diagram somebody believes from one they can argue with.
#
# Joinable only through a key that resolves
#   A rule that declares `layer` and returns a claim address in that column names no layer, and this
#   join then drops those pairs, can shade most of the graph as thinly checked, and is wrong about
#   which. `checks/grain.sqlc`'s `unresolvable` arm is what keeps it true.
#
# Both are NULL on the unit arm, for the order's reason
#   No rule declares a unit as its subject, so a unit is not thinly checked; it is outside what the
#   rules examine entirely. A zero would claim that somebody looked; the blank says nobody could.
#
# The nodes are the edges' ends, not every layer
#   That is right, and worth saying. Not every filed layer is in the layer graph: a layer in no
#   part, in either direction, is in no composition and is rightly not a node. Taking the nodes from
#   `layers/every_layer.sqlc` instead would draw a graph that says every layer takes part in a
#   composition, which is the drawing asserting what the model denies.
-- rank/graph_edges.sqlc's ends, with evaluation_order and layer_reach joined at (filing, layer).
SELECT 'layers' AS graph,
       e.filing || '/' || e.layer AS node,
       r.rank                     AS rank,
       c.examined_by              AS examined_by,
       c.violated                 AS violated
FROM (
    SELECT p.composition AS filing, p.composed_layer AS layer
    FROM ( :compose(composition/parts.sqlc) ) p
    UNION
    SELECT p.part_filing, p.part_layer
    FROM ( :compose(composition/parts.sqlc) ) p
) e
LEFT JOIN (
    :compose(rank/evaluation_order.sqlc)
) r ON r.filing = e.filing AND r.layer = e.layer
LEFT JOIN (
    :compose(rank/layer_reach.sqlc)
) c ON c.filing = e.filing AND c.layer = e.layer
UNION ALL
SELECT 'units', u.unit, NULL, NULL, NULL
FROM (
    SELECT c.from_unit AS unit FROM ( :compose(units/conversions.sqlc) ) c
    UNION
    SELECT c.to_unit        FROM ( :compose(units/conversions.sqlc) ) c
) u
